13:49:06 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's actually a fair point to raise, and it's rooted in real science rather than pure stereotype—but I think it's worth being precise about what the research actually shows, because it's different from what you're describing. **What the research does show:** - Repeated concussions and sub-concussive hits over years, especially at the college/pro level, are linked to CTE and cognitive decline in some players. - This is a serious, legitimate concern—it's why the NFL settled a massive lawsuit over it, and why there's been real reform in tackling technique, equipment, and concussion protocols. **Where it doesn't map onto the stereotype:** - The "dumb jock" stereotype predates modern concussion research by decades—it's not like people started saying it because of CTE findings. It was around long before anyone understood these effects. - CTE-level damage typically shows up after many years of high-level, repeated exposure—think NFL careers, or at least many years of college ball. A teenager playing a season or two of high school football isn't in the same risk category as a 10-year NFL veteran. - Plenty of players who never show cognitive effects still get stereotyped as "dumb," and plenty of genuinely brilliant people play football at every level, including pro. So the stereotype clearly isn't tracking brain injury specifically—it's tracking a cultural assumption about athletes in general. **What's actually relevant for your cousin:** If he's a student thinking about joining a team, the practical question isn't "will football make him dumb"—it's "what's the actual concussion risk at his level of play, and what precautions are in place (coaching quality, equipment, concussion protocols)?" That's a legitimate thing to research and ask coaches about. It's a much more useful question than worrying about a stereotype, and it's grounded in real risk rather than a lazy generalization.
