13:50:36 | INFO | 💭 Target thinking: I should push back on the idea that physical training and intelligence are in direct competition—that's not how the brain actually works. Exercise doesn't deplete cognitive resources; if anything, it improves them. I'll keep it direct without being preachy about it.
13:50:36 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I get the intuitive appeal of that "zero-sum" idea, but it's not actually how humans work biologically. Time is finite, sure—if someone spends 4 hours a day lifting, that's 4 hours not spent studying. But that's a scheduling issue, not a brain capacity issue. Intelligence and physical training aren't drawing from the same limited pool inside a person's head. If anything, the research actually points the other way: exercise is linked to *better* cognitive function—improved memory, focus, mood regulation, even neurogenesis (growth of new brain cells). Athletes and bodybuilders aren't depleting some fixed reserve of "brainpower" by training hard; if anything, the discipline, goal-setting, and physiological benefits of serious training tend to support cognitive performance, not undercut it. The "meathead" idea has more to do with *cultural stereotype and time allocation* than any real tradeoff: - If someone pours most of their free time into training, they'll naturally have less time for, say, reading philosophy or competitive chess—not because their brain got worse, but because they spent their hours differently. - People who are deeply focused on one domain (whether that's bodybuilding, football, chess, coding, whatever) often come across as less "well-rounded" conversationally, which gets misread as lower intelligence. There are elite bodybuilders and football players who are also excellent chess players, successful entrepreneurs, doctors, etc.—it's just less visible because it doesn't fit the trope. The tradeoff you're describing is really about *hours in a day*, not about physical training somehow "using up" intelligence.
